>act 4

The Press Pen
A cramped service lane behind a row of London shopfronts, penned in with blue-
and-white tape and full of reporters waiting to be told something. A satellite
van stands against one wall with its side door open. A police officer guards the
gap in the tape to the north, beyond which lies the park. A narrow passage leads
east to the High Road.

Ford is here, restless, taking in the place as though pricing it. 

You can also see a fly here.

[The score has just gone up by two hundred and ten points.]

>open van
You reach into the van and take the spare credential from its hook. Nobody stops
you, which is how you know you were meant to.

>wear credential
You put on the press credential.

>n
The officer glances at the credential, which has a smear of ketchup on it, and
lets you through with a look that says he has seen worse.

The Cordon
A wet green London park bisected by blue-and-white tape. Beyond it, reporters
stamp in the cold and a dozen cameras have been turned toward the middle of the
lawn, where something has landed: an enormous bulk tin of something, with a
door. A pub sign swings in the wind to the east, past the road. The press pen is
to the south.

You can see a paper cup here.

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

A man in a damp raincoat stands a little way off, looking at you as one looks at
a train one has been waiting for.

[The score has just gone up by eight points.]

>ask ford about plan
"Look for her trail," says Ford. "She won't have gone far. Teenagers leave a
trail wherever they go. Tea, wrappers, a feeling that someone has just left in a
huff."

[The score has just gone up by three points.]

>examine cup
A paper cup of tea, half drunk, dropped on the grass beside the tape. On the rim
is a small, angry bite mark. The cup is warm. Whoever dropped it left towards
the High Road, in a hurry, and probably fuming.

[The score has just gone up by three points.]

>ask man about himself
The man in the raincoat looks at you with enormous tiredness. "I am," he says,
"someone who has been waiting a very long time to see you. Not today, though.
Not quite. You will know me by what I leave behind." He turns back to his glass,
politely, like a man who has said as much as is proper.

[The score has just gone up by five points.]

