>e

The Studio Floor
A cavernous studio of cameras on trolleys and cables like a thicket. In the
middle stands a glossy set with a curved desk and a sign: ON AIR. A big autocue
screen at the back is scrolling someone else's words. The lobby is west and the
green room east.

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

>ask ford about plan
"If we need to talk to Tricia, we do it properly," says Ford. "And if we need to
talk to a lot of people at once, this is a television studio. It was built to
make people look at a camera."

[The score has just gone up by three points.]

>e

The Green Room
A cramped, comfortable room with a sofa, a kettle, a dish of unopened biscuits
and a mirror ringed with lights. It is, for reasons that have never been
explained, entirely beige. The studio floor is west.

Tricia McMillan sits on the arm of the sofa, watching you, and the girl beside
her, as if each might do something odd. 

Random lounges against the wall with her arms folded, looking at everything the
way a cat looks at a bath. 

You can also see a Random's diary here.

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

>take diary
Taken.

>ask tricia about broadcast
"Why would I give you air?" says Tricia. "I don't know who you are yet."

>tell tricia about the party in islington and the man with two heads
Tricia is quiet for a long moment. "I did not leave that party," she says at
last, almost to herself. "Everyone said I would have, and I thought not, and now
a stranger in a dressing gown tells me about the man with two heads, who I only
ever read about." She breathes out, a long shaky breath, like a cork coming out.
"All right. All right. I believe you. I'd like to believe I'm wrong, but I
don't."

In your pocket, the cube clears its throat. "RANDOM," it says, aloud, in a warm,
patient, impossible voice. "THERE YOU ARE."

Random goes white. She is on her feet and through the door before anyone can
stop her, her bag over one shoulder, and the sound of her footsteps echoes away
down the studio floor. "She runs," says Tricia, shakily. "Is that normal?" "It
is lately," says Ford.

[The score has just gone up by twelve points.]

