Platform 2
A long curved platform, tiled white, with a draught that smells of other people.
A train waits with its doors open, ready to go somewhere. The entrance is east.

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

Tricia follows, taking in the place with a journalist's eye.

Random is standing at the open door of the train with one foot on the step, as
if she could not decide what she was running from. "Don't," she says, before you
have said anything. You do not. You stand there, on the platform, in your
dressing gown, and wait, which turns out to be the right thing. At last she
steps back. "Fine," she says. "But I'm not talking about it."

[The score has just gone up by ten points.]

>ask random about herself
"You're the first person who's asked," says Random, as if tallying. "I like
patterns. I hate being a pattern. I came here because everything the cube said
led here, and I wanted to see whether it was lying. It wasn't. That's the worst
part."

[The score has just gone up by five points.]

>ask random about her mother
"She doesn't know me," says Random, of Tricia, watching her across the room.
"She looks at me as if I'm a theory. My mother looks at me like I'm a deadline.
I don't know which is worse."

>apologise to random
"For what?" says Random. You tell her: for not being there, for the cube, for
sandwiches as a substitute for conversation. She looks at you for a long moment.
"That was a lot," she says, which for her is a hug.

[The score has just gone up by five points.]

>give diary to random
Random takes the diary, checks the cover, checks the spine, and looks at you
with sudden, wary respect. "You didn't open it," she says. It isn't a question.
For the first time since you met, she seems to have lowered something.

